 State Election – Tuesday, November 3, 2026 
 Polls will be open 7 am – 8 pm.  
 Last day to register to vote is Saturday, October 24th between the hours of 9-5. 
 Information for Voters - 2026 Ballot Questions Sample Ballot for November 3 Election   
 Vote by Mail -  The State has sent out Vote by mail applications to anyone who hadn't earlier applied to vote by mail in the State election.   If you aren't interested in this option, you can disregard the application.   The deadline to return a Vote by Mail application is October 27th at 5 pm.  We anticipate receiving the ballots to begin mailing out at the end of September.  Ballots can be returned in person, in the Ballot Drop Box which will be set up outside Town Hall, or by US mail.  In-person early voting will take place October 17 – October 30.  Where Do I Vote? 

 Both precincts vote at  Groveland Town Hall  183 Main Street Groveland, MA 01834 
 Polls are open from 7 am until 8 pm. 
 You must be registered to vote 10 days prior to Town Meeting (April 27, 2026) to be eligible to participate and vote in the Municipal Election on May 4th.  

 Voter Registration You can register to vote in the Town Clerk's office during regular office hours.  You can register to vote or check your registration status on the Secretary of State's website.  
 Election Security 
 There are some basic facts all citizens of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts should understand. Every community in this state, no matter its size, uses some form of paper ballot. There are no touch-screen terminals connected to the internet and every ballot provides every vote with a paper trail rendering your vote unable to be hacked or changed. If any question arises about the results we report on election night (the so-called “preliminary results”), we can pull the ballots and count them by hand.   As is with every election in Groveland, all election workers are sworn in, Wardens and Constables are present with the ballots throughout the day, and the entire Recount process can be utilized if necessary. 
 The Town of Groveland uses ImageCast 2 Precinct Tabulators for both  precincts.  The machines were purchased from and are maintained by LHS Associates. The voting machines we use are one of two possible models certified by the Secretary of State’s Election Division.  Our current voting machines were chosen via a competitive quote process, and are always tested in our office prior to an Election Day (testing times are posted and open to the public for viewing).  Machines are also secured throughout the process in the same way as the ballots.  They are not connected to the internet, have no wired connections other than to a power outlet (with battery backup, also tested in advance).  The machines generate a “zero tape” which is posted on the morning of each election, showing that each machine starts with no count prior to the beginning of the election.  A final vote count tape at the end of the night is compared to the number of persons checked in to vote to make sure that there are no discrepancies.  
 Finally, the state’s Voter Registration Information System (“VRIS”) is a closed system, also not connected to the internet.  Voter Registration itself, the lists of registered voters that we generate for election day, and all election-related data, are contained in that closed system, accessible only via a private line circuit from our office to Boston, an arrangement duplicated in every city and town in the Commonwealth, so that we are connected to that system rather than via an internet IP address connection.   Residents with any questions can call the office anytime, 978-556-7221, visit our website, or call the Secretary of State’s Elections Division toll free at 1-800-462-VOTE. 

